  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - We propose a multi-level optimization strategy that combines periodicity with non-uniform assignments\n   - The approach leverages the observation that periodic patterns often appear in optimal solutions\n   - We start from a given parent solution and explore periodic perturbations of various lengths\n   - This method is commonly used in minimax correlation problems and has shown empirical success\n   - The strategy addresses the high-dimensional optimization challenge by introducing structural assumptions\n   - Unlike simple random initialization, this method exploits problem-specific patterns\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: Piecewise constant functions with up to 100 potential kinks\n   - Periodic signal: A base signal of length L is repeated at integer shifts\n   - Folding mechanism: The solution is folded onto [0,2] while controlling overlap penalties\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: \n     - 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n     - \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n   - Transformations:\n     - Standardize: (h_i - min_i)/(max_i - min_i)\n     - Apply periodicity: h_i = f(i) + g(i) trades higher frequency content\n   - The optimization minimizes the maximum correlation using the island model\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Feature extraction from parent solution:\n     - Compute min/max values\n     - Determine key transition points\n     - Set base length and flexibility variant\n\n   - Periodic signal construction:\n     - Choose prime candidate shifts: {p | p = k/280, k \u2208 1..50}\n     - Generate base signal with periodicity\n     - Apply fold-back to [0,2] domain\n\n   - Optimization phases:\n     - Multilevel: From small to large populations (phase 1-3)\n     - Iterative repair of constraint violations\n     - Smart truncation when constraints are unbalanced\n\n   - Cross-entropy integration:\n     - Use target frequencies as mutation directions\n     - Learn optimal periodic parameters\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Population management:\n     - Always test at least 10 distinct parents\n     - $5^{10}$ total variation points\n     - Stop when improvement < 1e-6\n\n   - Periodic module types:\n     - Triangular wave (smooth edges)\n     - Square wave (abrupt changes)\n     - Sawtooth wave (linear ramps)\n\n   - Mutation strategies:\n     - Adaptive shift magnitude: Based on signal complexity\n     - Parametric inversion: $h\u2192 (1-h)$ used in phases 2-5\n     - Wavelet domain perturbation: Applied to periodic signals\n\n5. Computational Budget and Robustness\n   - Explicit 1-minute (60 seconds) hard limit\n   - Three distinct approaches:\n     1. Pure periodic search\n     2. Non-periodic optimization\n     3. Hybrid periodic with smooth transition\n   - Check candidate feasibility five times per minute\n   - Fallback: Return best periodic solution if timely branch fails\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Perform exhaustive checks:\n     - Integral balance: |sum(h) - n_points/2| < 1e-5\n     - Feasibility: 0 \u2264 all(h[i]) \u2264 1\n     - Correlation recomputation with different modes\n   - Final selection: Choose least overlapped solution\n   - Return format: (h, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nImplementation ensures the function:\n   - Defines a valid piecewise solution with 100 kinks\n   - Subdivides domain in harmonic ratio\n   - Balances within-interval assignments\n   - Maintains periodicity without fractional indexing\n\nThe method explicitly handles the initial parent with conditional growth:\n   - Start from core parent components\n   - Increase complexity until no further improvement\n   - Preserve integral constraints through enforced transitions\n\nThis approach statistically should find solutions matching or exceeding current records by systematically searching periodic variations with adaptive parameterization.",
